\n\n#### 1. The Problem with Linear Search\nIf you had to look for a book by checking every shelf one by one, it would take a long time. This is like searching through a list.\n\n#### 2. The Hash Map Solution\nA hash map uses a **hash function** to assign each book a unique \"address\" or \"slot\" in the library.\n\n- **Key**: The title of the book (e.g., \"Harry Potter\").\n- **Value**: The actual book.\n- **Hash Function**: A mathematical formula that takes the title \"Harry Potter\" and turns it into a number, say `42`.\n- **Storage**: The book is placed in slot `42`.\n\n#### 3. Retrieving Data\nTo find \"Harry Potter,\" you:\n1. Run the hash function on \"Harry Potter\" → get `42`.\n2. Go directly to slot `42`.\n3. Grab the book.\n\nThis is **instant** (O(1) time complexity), regardless of how many books are in", "client": {"ttft_s": 0.6483313999997335, "decode_s": 4.853369000004022, "total_s": 5.502009000003454, "n_gen": 256, "n_chunks": 256, "tg": 52.540822673855764, "pp": 46.27263155850901}}
