User Datagram Protocol), we must look at their fundamental design philosophies: **TCP is designed for reliability**, while **UDP is designed for speed and efficiency**.\n\nHere is a detailed comparison based on your specific criteria.\n\n### 1. Guarantees\n\n| Feature | TCP (Transmission Control Protocol) | UDP (User Datagram Protocol) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Reliability** | **Guaranteed.** TCP ensures that data is delivered exactly as sent. | **No guarantee.** UDP sends packets without checking if they arrive. |\n| **Ordering** | **Guaranteed.** Packets are reassembled in the exact order they were sent. | **No guarantee.** Packets may arrive out of order or not at all. |\n| **Connection** | **Connection-oriented.** Requires a handshake (SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK) before data transfer. | **Connectionless.** No handshake; data is sent immediately. |\n| **Flow Control** | **Yes.** Uses sliding windows to prevent overwhelming the receiver. | **No.** The sender sends as fast as the network allows. |\n\n### 2. Typical Use Cases\n\n", "client": {"ttft_s": 0.6096036000017193, "decode_s": 4.708541599997261, "total_s": 5.318431400002737, "n_gen": 256, "n_chunks": 256, "tg": 54.156896479399975, "pp": 49.212307801193084}}
